WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.565
Estimate a rental as a complete session.

00:00:02.565 --> 00:00:06.525
Include provisioning, package installation, model transfer, data preparation,

00:00:06.525 --> 00:00:09.500
active computation, evaluation, troubleshooting, and cleanup.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.230
Every running minute may be billed at the full accelerator rate,

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.495
even when the GPU is idle.

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:17.500
Multiply the rate by realistic elapsed time rather than ideal compute time,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.660
then include CPU, memory,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.700
and operating-system surcharges if the provider prices them separately.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.529
Storage has an independent lifecycle.

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:27.660
Persistent disks, snapshots, model caches, datasets, logs,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.000
and object storage can remain billable after an instance stops.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.500
Data transfer may be charged when files cross regions or leave the provider.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.465
Place compute near required data, avoid copying artifacts unnecessarily,

00:00:38.465 --> 00:00:42.000
and understand whether stopping an instance retains its disks and addresses.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.665
Use on-demand rental for uncertain or short work.

00:00:44.665 --> 00:00:48.240
Consider reserved capacity only when utilization is predictable enough to justify the

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:48.975
commitment.

00:00:48.975 --> 00:00:52.700
Use interruptible capacity for jobs that save checkpoints, resume safely,

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.500
and tolerate delayed completion.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.270
A low spot price is not a saving if repeated interruption loses hours of work or corrupts

00:00:58.270 --> 00:00:59.500
an unprotected output.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.970
Set project budgets, spend alerts, quotas, and automatic expiration where available.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.205
Label resources with an owner and purpose.

00:01:06.205 --> 00:01:09.860
Schedule development shutdowns and use job wrappers that release compute after both

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.000
success and failure.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.995
Alerts may notify rather than stop spending, so assign someone to respond.

00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:18.750
At the end of each session, review compute, storage, snapshots, public addresses,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.444
and transfer.

00:01:19.444 --> 00:01:23.490
Shutdown discipline is an engineering control, not a reminder to be careful.
